{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Moses spoke so to the children of Israel, etc.</b> There are several difficulties in this account. For 'shortness of spirit' means hard labor, so why did Scripture distinguish between them? Furthermore, what is said afterward — ״בֹּא דַבֵּר אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ (Exodus 6:11) — implies that this comes to remedy the refusal and rejection of Israel, and the matter lacks explanation. And everyone asks: surely this a fortiori argument has a refutation, for what is the case with Israel, who indeed had shortness of spirit? And what did the Holy One, blessed be He, answer him regarding this a fortiori argument, and how did He remedy it with what is said: ״וַיְצַוֵּם אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. (Exodus 6:13)?" "And it appears to me regarding this, that the entire passage from ״וָאֵרָא אֶל אַבְרָהָם״ to ״וְנָתַתִּי אוֹתָהּ לָכֶם מוֹרָשָׁה״ makes no mention of Pharaoh at all — that he should send them from his land. From this it is understood as though HaShem wanted them to leave on their own without the king's decree (הורמנא דמלכא), for so the language implies: ״וְהוֹצֵאתִי״ ״וְלָקַחְתִּי״. And even though He had already said to Pharaoh ״שַׁלַּח עַמִּי וְיַעַבְדֻנִי״, perhaps after Pharaoh's refusal HaShem wanted them to leave there against Pharaoh's will. And Moses erred in this matter, for he should have added on his own understanding and spoken to Israel also this time that Pharaoh would send them, for he is coerced until he says 'I am willing,' for He is not a man, blessed be He, to change His mind. But Moses added nothing at all; rather, he spoke to them the words of HaShem, blessed be He, exactly as they were, as it is written ״וַיְדַבֵּר מֹשֶׁה כֵּן אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — 'כֵּן' indicates the words exactly as they were, and ״לֹא שָׁמְעוּ אֶל מֹשֶׁה״ — they did not accept his words. And they should have answered his words: 'How shall we leave without the king's permission?' — in the manner of what is said (1 Samuel 16:2) ״אֵיךְ אֵלֵךְ וְשָׁמַע שָׁאוּל וַהֲרָגָנִי״. Indeed, from shortness of spirit — their breathing was short, like a panicked person — therefore they could not bring forth their answer to him from their mouths. And furthermore they feared the hard labor, lest it weigh upon them as it weighed upon them during Moses's first mission. And when HaShem saw the disposition of Israel and the cause of their refusal and rejection, He wanted to remedy their error: ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה: בֹּא דַבֵּר אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ etc. For even from the outset it did not arise in His thought, blessed be He, that they would go without permission — except that Moses and Israel both erred regarding His earlier words. And here too, in these words, Moses erred. For he assumed (מדתלה השליחות) the mission regarding Pharaoh, which implies that specifically the will of the sender is what is required, but not the will of Israel — for with a strong hand He will drive them out against their will. Therefore he said ״הֵן בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא שָׁמְעוּ אֵלַי״ — not by way of an a fortiori argument, but he said: in my opinion they do not wish to listen to leave Egypt at all, for we find in our Sages of blessed memory regarding the plague of darkness that there were many wicked among Israel who did not want to leave, and so Rashi explained in parashat Bo (Exodus 10:22). And even though the verse tells us the truth — that from shortness of spirit they did not listen — nevertheless Moses supposed they did not listen because they had no desire to leave at all. Therefore Moses said, without an a fortiori argument: since the children of Israel did not listen to me and have no desire to leave, if so how will Pharaoh listen to me to drive them out? For Pharaoh will take their refusal and rejection as his exoneration. And when HaShem saw the error of Moses and the error of Israel, He wanted to remedy both of them together, as it is said: ״וַיְצַוֵּם אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְאֶל פַּרְעֹה מֶלֶךְ מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 6:13) — that there should be here the will of the sender and the will of the one sent, for the one is not without the other. For he shall speak to the children of Israel that they should be willing to leave, for he shall speak to their heart good and comforting words; and to Pharaoh, that he should be willing to send them. And those of Israel who did not want to leave died in the three days of darkness. And as for his repeating and saying ״אֵיךְ יִשְׁמָעֵנִי פַרְעֹה וַאֲנִי עֲרַל שְׂפָתָיִם״ (Exodus 6:12) — because the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״בֹּא דַבֵּר אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ (Exodus 6:11), he thought that perhaps HaShem had retracted what He said, ״וְהָיָה הוּא יִהְיֶה לְךָ לְפֶה״ (Exodus 4:16), and now wants that I myself shall speak everything and not Aaron. Therefore he said: behold, I am uncircumcised of lips. To this it was said ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְאֶל אַהֲרֹן וַיְצַוֵּם״ etc. (Exodus 6:13) — indicating that He had not retracted from this, and Aaron would be his mouth, and thereby He would remedy everything." "<b>(Continuation of the previous comment — this is the second reading embedded within comment [1].)</b> And here too, in these words, Moses erred, for he assumed the mission regarding Pharaoh, which implies that specifically the will of the sender is required, but not the will of Israel, for with a strong hand He will drive them out against their will. Therefore he said ״הֵן בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא שָׁמְעוּ אֵלַי״ — not by way of an a fortiori argument, but he said: in my opinion they do not wish to listen to leave Egypt at all, for we find in our Sages of blessed memory regarding the plague of darkness that there were many wicked among Israel who did not want to leave, and so Rashi explained in parashat Bo (Exodus 10:22). And even though the verse tells us the truth — that from shortness of spirit they did not listen — nevertheless Moses supposed they did not listen because they have no desire to leave at all. Therefore Moses said, without an a fortiori argument: since the children of Israel did not listen to me and have no desire to leave, if so how will Pharaoh listen to me to drive them out? For Pharaoh will take their refusal and rejection as his exoneration. And when HaShem saw the error of Moses and the error of Israel, He wanted to remedy both of them together, as it is said: ״וַיְצַוֵּם אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל וְאֶל פַּרְעֹה מֶלֶךְ מִצְרָיִם״ (Exodus 6:13) — that there should be here the will of the sender and the will of the one sent, for the one is not without the other. For he shall speak to the children of Israel that they should be willing to leave, for he shall speak to their heart good and comforting words; and to Pharaoh, that he should be willing to send them. And those of Israel who did not want to leave died in the three days of darkness. And as for his repeating and saying ״אֵיךְ יִשְׁמָעֵנִי פַרְעֹה וַאֲנִי עֲרַל שְׂפָתָיִם״ (Exodus 6:12) — because the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him ״בֹּא דַבֵּר אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ (Exodus 6:11), he thought that perhaps HaShem had retracted what He had said, ״וְהָיָה הוּא יִהְיֶה לְךָ לְפֶה״ (Exodus 4:16), and now wants that I myself shall speak everything and not Aaron. Therefore he said: behold, I am uncircumcised of lips. To this it was said ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְאֶל אַהֲרֹן וַיְצַוֵּם״ etc. (Exodus 6:13) — indicating that He had not retracted from this and Aaron would be his mouth, and thereby He would remedy everything." "And according to its plain sense, we may say as well that there is no refutation to this a fortiori argument, for Moses did not understand their intent — that they did not listen from shortness of spirit — and he supposed that they did not listen to him because they do not believe in his prophecy, and if so all the more so will Pharaoh not believe him. But the Holy One, blessed be He, knew the truth — that from shortness of spirit they did not listen; therefore He did not bother to answer him anything at all regarding the a fortiori argument, for the Holy One, blessed be He, knew that there is no substance to this a fortiori argument, and He answered him only regarding his saying ״וַאֲנִי עֲרַל שְׂפָתָיִם״, and said that Aaron would be his interpreter as stated. And as for His saying a second time at the end of the matter, ״הֵן אֲנִי עֲרַל שְׂפָתַיִם״ — because it was said ״דַּבֵּר אֶל פַּרְעֹה מֶלֶךְ מִצְרַיִם אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי דֹּבֵר אֵלֶיךָ״, Moses thought that what HaShem would speak to him and to Aaron — this is the speech for which Aaron would be his mouth; but what HaShem would speak to him alone, he himself alone would need to speak to Pharaoh, for so the language implies: ״אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי דֹּבֵר אֵלֶיךָ״. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him ״אַתָּה תְדַבֵּר״ etc. ״וְאַהֲרֹן אָחִיךָ יְדַבֵּר אֶל פַּרְעֹה״ — all the speeches, even what I speak to you alone."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {1 "The word מדתלה appears to be from Aramaic מדתלא ('to lean/assume'); rendered as 'assumed' to preserve the sense of Moses's mistaken inference.", 2 "This comment [2] reproduces the second half of the Hebrew text of comment [1]; it appears the source provided the same continuous derashah split across two indices with [2] beginning mid-essay at 'And here too, in these words, Moses erred.' Translated as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:01.611252738Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}